### Import pino-http (CommonJS and ESM)

Source: https://github.com/pinojs/pino-http/blob/master/_autodocs/api-reference/pinoHttp.md

Shows how to import the package in CommonJS and TypeScript/ESM environments. The default export and named pinoHttp export are available, along with stdSerializers and startTime.

```javascript
// CommonJS
const pinoHttp = require('pino-http')
const { pinoHttp } = require('pino-http')
const { stdSerializers, startTime } = require('pino-http')

// TypeScript / ESM interop
import pinoHttp from 'pino-http'
import { pinoHttp, HttpLogger, Options } from 'pino-http'
```

--------------------------------

### Delegating to a downstream handler with next

Source: https://github.com/pinojs/pino-http/blob/master/_autodocs/api-reference/http-logger.md

Shows Express-style usage where pino-http is used as middleware and the next callback continues the chain. The logger is invoked with req, res, and next.

```javascript
// Express-style: wrap an existing middleware chain
const logger = require('pino-http')()
const app = require('express')()

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  logger(req, res, next) // chain continues via next()
})

app.get('/', (req, res) => {
  req.log.info('route handler')
  res.send('hello world')
})
```

